WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.700
- Michael did actually cut
to the heart of the problem

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.100
but I approach the same problem

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.130
from a somewhat different perspective

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.470
really looking for more

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:21.480
by my experience in their
arms control negotiations,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.330
and when you sit at the table

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.943
things look quite different often.

00:00:30.474 --> 00:00:32.370
I don't see many students in the audience

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.580
but for those who are present
that can tell you that

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.730
Uh, that books like

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.210
Getting To Yes

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.310
don't actually reflect anything

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.253
that goes on at the negotiating table.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.110
Yes it's a great book to teach,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.510
but it's not a good book to follow

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.220
as you negotiate treaties.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.780
So I would say that yes

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.153
we are at a really critical juncture.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.930
Maybe we're already
actually past the juncture.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:11.260
Honestly as I do not see much
hope in saving the existing

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.870
network of arms control treaties.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.910
I don't see much hope for extending even

00:01:19.776 --> 00:01:21.413
the new star treaty.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.830
I think we're entering the world of

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.163
an unregulated arms race.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.290
Maybe not so much in numbers

00:01:36.491 --> 00:01:39.700
as in on new capabilities,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.923
but these new capabilities
make war more likely.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.830
Let me cut at this problem

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:50.890
at several levels.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.403
On the more technical levels

00:01:57.524 --> 00:01:59.600
or the arms control actual level is that

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.290
well if you look at the
INF treaty, especially,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.560
I don't see much use
for existing procedures

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.943
and mechanisms to resolve concerns.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.320
To me the situation

00:02:16.982 --> 00:02:21.473
with the Russian missile
violating the INF treaty or other;

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.363
the U.S.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.240
accusations of such violation.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.080
Well, in fact, it looks quite similar.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.900
We had similar cases before

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.780
while I was still at the foreign ministry.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.260
And let me tell you back

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.720
yeah, that's like already
many years ago, but back then

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.853
similar issues were resolved
in a very different way.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.420
The way it was tackled
by both sides on nowadays

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.163
simply did not allow for
any resolution whatsoever.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.090
That's really why the treaty collapsed.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:07.120
I do not really try to claim that I know

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.450
for sure whether there
was a violation or not

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.653
and frankly it actually doesn't matter.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.025
What really matters from my view,

00:03:18.025 --> 00:03:21.210
from a practitioner's point of view,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.600
is the fact that there was
a chance to resolve that

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.363
and the chance was not taken.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.200
Second; a big issue now
sort of the higher level.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.860
The big issue is

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.843
so what do we address in arms control?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.820
That has been the really
fundamental difference

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.310
that really came all to the floor

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:52.230
especially all clearly during the Obama

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:55.633
years.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.250
Do we deal with nuclear weapons?

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.123
or do we deal with military capabilities?

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:08.030
The United States really tried very hard

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.198
to reduce nuclear weapons, yes,
that was the Prague speech.

00:04:11.198 --> 00:04:14.390
And everything

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:15.840
to which Russia actually said

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.543
but nuclear weapons is just
one part of military balance.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.100
Why don't we tackle things

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.963
like missile defense and long
range conventional weapons?

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.770
Oh, the difference is to an
extent actually philosophical,

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:38.970
but also quite practical.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.580
During world war II the humankind

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.543
lost about 50 million people.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.210
Using weapons that were
really quite primitive

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.563
from today's point of view.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.193
So, um.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.610
If we just eliminate nuclear weapons

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:03.040
do we save the humankind
or do we open gates for

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.773
a new conventional global war?

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:10.970
We don't have an answer.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.065
I'm not trying to answer it.

00:05:13.065 --> 00:05:14.803
I'm just saying that we
need to think about that.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.720
All the trends are not very optimistic.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:29.380
Yes let me be actually very
cynical here and even brutal.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.530
Yes at the end of the cold war

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.207
and for the following 25 years

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:39.825
the United States did possess the monopoly

00:05:39.825 --> 00:05:42.630
on advanced conventional weapons.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.220
We talk two Wars in the Gulf,

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.853
we talk the Balkans, we talk Libya.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.030
Well it was really wonderful world where

00:05:51.911 --> 00:05:54.100
it was possible to reduce
reliance on nuclear weapons,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:57.310
because we got wonderful non-nuclear

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.223
capabilities.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.480
Well really let's go back to the 70's.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.173
The war in Vietnam ended.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.640
He said it was a defeat;
he said the United States

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.670
would not really go to
war for a very long time.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:22.210
Why? Because the traditional
or conventional forces

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.350
were unusable, yes, the Soviet union lived

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.163
through the same
experience in Afghanistan.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.350
I would say that after
Vietnam and Afghanistan

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:35.310
the world entered

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.410
a qualitatively new

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.900
stage.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.163
The stage of peaceful foreign policy.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:51.030
The stage when Clausewitz
was actually wrong

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.943
because war could no
longer be continuation.

00:06:59.494 --> 00:07:01.050
So policy by other means, yes,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.940
unfortunately that stage
was very short-lived,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.190
because now we got advanced
conventional weapons

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.830
quite precise on not
much collateral damage.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.570
We don't go to the battlefield.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:19.963
Yes and things are really wonderful.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:24.760
What happened in 2015;
it was the first use

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:30.063
of similar weapons by Russia
on battlefield in Syria.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.570
So the monopoly was lost.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:33.900
What do we see now?

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:38.610
Nuclear air launch cruise missiles

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.310
low yield warheads for Trident

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:46.960
look and things like that.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.770
The Suwalki Gap like in Europe

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:53.770
and generally big Russian
Military threat to NATO.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.010
Well I think it's
important all to understand

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:04.703
all the nuclear capabilities
and conventional capabilities,

00:08:12.215 --> 00:08:13.800
Oh I'm sorry,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.770
are universally related.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.690
Well, in fact, Russia now all says no

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.360
we now got conventional deterrents

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.140
we don't have to rely on nuclear weapons.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.100
That's really wonderful.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.463
Yes, well, somehow NATO does
not see that as wonderful.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.440
So, yes, that's kind of
all the second level.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.340
Isn't the third level
that I think needs to

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:39.310
be addressed to understand why
we see all the dismantlement

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.520
of the arms control framework.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.230
Is the broader concept
of international law,

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:47.493
international regimes,
and multilateralism.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.507
The dominant perception in the west,

00:08:51.507 --> 00:08:53.890
not just the United States,

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.340
is that we are good guys,

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.060
because we are for international law

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:03.060
and multi-lateral approaches.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.810
Even Russia is not.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.310
Well that's actually not true.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.650
Well in fact Russia is a conservative

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.280
international law

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.800
multilateralist country.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.500
Yes, it adheres to
conservative old, you know,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:27.500
kind of fundamentals of
international law; Sovereign States.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.450
You're not bound by international regime

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:36.500
unless you express who's agreed to that

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.200
for treaty to enter into force

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.570
everyone should sign

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:44.683
and things like that.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.550
He's in, he's in the West
after the end of the cold war

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.190
it was more like the
majority actually rules.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.023
So

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.280
the majority of States agree

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.770
that something is a good thing.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.520
So, yes, we perceive going

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:05.910
with that policy.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.420
Well just recall in the 90's

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.407
we developed a concept of
humanitarian intervention.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.863
Well it actually does violate
traditional international law.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:23.573
Yes that's a gap actually
in the body of law.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.880
Well, since it's for a very good reason.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.563
Well, let's actually go ahead.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.520
Some States might not support it, yes,

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:38.520
and veto it at the United
Nations security council,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.823
but we do it for all the common good.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:50.220
Uh, we go to war over Casola.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:55.210
Well, that actually did
violate the U.N. charter,

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.300
but it was for very good reasons.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.833
Yes and then we recognized
independence of Casola.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:07.250
In 2009 the United States
did file a fascinating legal

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.040
justification for that.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.300
Well, it was called the U.S. Legal Opinion

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.280
on The Independence of Casola.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.817
Surprise surprise in 2014, um,

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:30.040
Moscow took that document
and to the last word

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.510
implemented it in Crimea.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:35.170
Yes, but we don't accept that because

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.300
that was

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.303
that was for a bad reason.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.520
So, uh, we are into

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:51.310
this conflict between

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.563
two conceptions of international law.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.290
What as we have this fundamental conflict

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:04.470
and we don't really talk about it much

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:06.853
at least in the West.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:11.430
We do see and we are concerned

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:15.263
about what we see on the surface.

00:12:15.263 --> 00:12:16.680
On the surface is

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.320
the dismantlement

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:26.260
of the arms control regimes.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:28.893
Can we overcome that?

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.380
I think chances are low.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.643
Yes the chances are low because
we talk about all things

00:12:39.989 --> 00:12:41.870
all that are like on the surface.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.760
Like the threat of war in general

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:45.853
or nuclear war specifically.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:51.200
And we do not even try

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:53.703
to seek some kind

00:12:53.703 --> 00:12:57.090
of a common position on
the bigger questions.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:58.343
What's international law?

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:04.243
What is strategic stability?

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:10.653
What we should account for
as we write new treaties.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.963
As long as we do not address these things.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:18.803
Well,

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:22.360
always, I agree with you.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.280
I'll go to church put a candle,

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.773
yes, and hope well that we all survive.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.490
And I can tell you the down the road

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:32.740
survival will be

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:37.153
much more difficult.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:40.400
To be honest the Cold War

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:43.420
standoff

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:48.420
with nuclear weapons was
nice, stable, understandable.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:53.970
I would feel quite comfortable
right now to be honest

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:58.970
but we are entering
the era of new weapons,

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:01.373
of hypersonic weapons.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:08.180
when leaders will have
two or three minutes

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.053
to make a decision.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.723
Yes, in this world,

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.363
no, seriously.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.290
Back during the cold war

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:23.520
you see

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:28.440
the opponents missiles flying.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:33.113
Yes, and you got 20 to
40 minutes to think.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:39.243
To verify the warning, to
connect to the opponent,

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:42.943
and ask

00:14:44.591 --> 00:14:45.643
Oh what the hell is going?

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.803
Well, now you've got two or three minutes.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:54.490
So also you have to react on first warning

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:59.299
and what's, uh, the
likely mode of reaction?

00:14:59.299 --> 00:15:00.170
(thumb thumps on desk)

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:02.193
Yes it's actually the buttons, right.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.810
We lived through a similar period.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.697
A quite short period, though, in like 83'.

00:15:11.212 --> 00:15:13.860
When American short range

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:18.040
intermediate range missiles

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:21.593
in Europe could reach
Moscow in seven minutes.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.520
That challenge was resolved

00:15:31.799 --> 00:15:34.010
in 87' by the INF treaty.

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:40.670
Given the state of relationship
between the Western Russia

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:42.884
I do not see such a treaty

00:15:42.884 --> 00:15:46.803
or something similar in
the foreseeable future.

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:51.280
So let's enjoy life and pray.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:52.113
Thank you.

